{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794537419, "title": "threejs/neon-implosion \u00b7 ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Create a vivid pointer interactive abstract neon art solar system particle implosion, a 10 second looping\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  animation in three.js, as a single web page.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[42.74, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/solar-implosion.html)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[47.731, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the animation as one HTML file: `<workspace>/solar-implosion.html`. I haven't opened it in a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  browser, so it's untested. It loads three.js from the unpkg CDN, so you need to be online.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Open it in a browser. If your browser blocks the CDN imports on a `file://` page, run `python3 -m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  http.server` in that folder and open `http://localhost:8000/solar-implosion.html`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Scene:** a particle sun with a corona, eight planets, some with rings, an asteroid belt, orbit trails\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and a dust halo. Everything is drawn as additive-blended neon points with bloom.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **10-second loop:** orbits keep a steady pace for the first half. From about 5 to 8.6 seconds the whole\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  system spirals inward and collapses into a bright white-hot core, with camera shake and a flash. It then re-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  expands. The motion is periodic in the loop time, so it repeats with no visible jump. A thin progress bar\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  along the bottom shows where you are in the loop.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Pointer:** a neon ring cursor follows your mouse. It repels and swirls nearby particles and makes them\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  glow, and the camera tilts and orbits slightly as you move. Clicking sends a stronger pulse.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I left the central part of the implosion curve and the particle counts as they are, but both are easy to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  change if you want a faster collapse or a lighter load. The scene is about 60,000 particles, so a weak GPU\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  may struggle.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
