[656.035, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step 4 report: Challenge**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did.** I tested five assumptions against the real page, riskiest first. Two were wrong in a way that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  mattered, and I fixed both in `solar-implosion.html`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | # | Assumption | Test | Result |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 1 | The core looks the same at any screen size. | Rendered at 640, 1920 and 390 px wide and measured the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  core width. | **Wrong.** At 8.2s it spanned 21% of the width at 640px but 7% at 1920px, so the climax was\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  about a third as big on a desktop or Retina screen. The size was in raw device pixels, and the largest sizes\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  would also hit GPU point-size caps (Apple GPUs commonly cap near 511). **Fixed:** the core is now a screen-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  relative billboard. It measures 37.7% to 38.9% of the shorter side at all three sizes. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 2 | The scene frames well on any screen shape. | Rendered at 390×844 portrait and looked at the sheet. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Wrong before the fix**, since the camera distance was fixed. The camera now pulls back on tall screens,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and the whole system plus the shock sphere frame correctly. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 3 | Real time wraps at 10s, opening at 85%. | Ran with the real clock for 12s and read the progress bar. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Held.** The bar advanced 10% a second and wrapped at about 10s. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 4 | The click shockwave works. | Compared scripted frames with and without a click. | **Held.** The frames\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  differed by 33.8, far above noise. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 5 | The 3.0 noise floor is harmless. | Hid the progress bar and hint text and re-measured. | **Wrong.**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The floor is about 0.5, and the overlays caused the 3.0. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Found**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The blind spot was that the climax was resolution-dependent. I only found it by rendering at several\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sizes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - My earlier seam verdict was measured against a floor that was too high. I re-measured it cleanly: the seam\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  step is 3.69, and ordinary steps range from 0.86 to 8.03. The seam is not an outlier.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - There were no console errors in any run.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course.** I didn't re-check the pointer feel or frame rate. Those stay with you and your own\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  browser.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Snapshots are in `.ambition/first/` (the original), `steps/3/` and `steps/4/` (this version).\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[656.114, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 5 of pushing the work further: Range. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 5/8 · Range`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[707.742, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 5/8 · Range\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What this is really trying to do, as verbs.** It has to make a stranger stop scrolling and get pulled in.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  It has to build tension and then pay it off with an impact worth replaying. It has to let the viewer feel\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  they're physically touching the thing, and loop without a seam. It must also not hurt, break or trap anyone,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  which matters because it's a flashing, fullscreen, pointer-hiding page. The genre's own verbs are *wow*,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  *build tension*, *release* and *respond to the pointer*.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fields and what I borrow from each**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Field | What they actually do | Direction I take |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Demoscene and motion-graphics artist** (the genre's own best practitioner) | Time the edit, with a held\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  breath before impact so the hit lands harder. | A 0.35s \"inhale\" just before the detonation. Particles dim,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  but the core doesn't, then the hit lands. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Colourist** | Keep a controlled palette and avoid muddy mid-tones. The collapse frames read as beige\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  haze, not neon. | Recolour the singularity as a white-hot centre with a violet halo, so the climax is neon.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Game-feel designer** | Every input gets immediate, legible feedback. | The cursor ring ripples on click,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and the hint text adapts to touch. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Sound designer** (the field I'd normally skip here) | Contrast comes from silence before the hit, which\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  is where the \"inhale\" above comes from. They also tie impacts to sound. | I borrowed only the silence-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  before-impact principle. Real audio would be a new feature I can't judge by ear, so I'm not building it. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Red team: accessibility and failure-mode tester** | Check photosensitivity (WCAG flash rules), `prefers-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reduced-motion`, what happens when the CDN or WebGL fails, and whether the page can trap someone. | See\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  below. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge I'm taking:** the impact moment people will replay, with the held breath and neon singularity,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  made safe enough for anyone to watch and share.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Red-team findings, tested for real or by reading the page**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Photosensitivity.** There is one bright flash per 10s loop, which is under the 3-per-second limit. I'm\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  measuring the actual luminance jumps below.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Reduced motion.** There was no `prefers-reduced-motion` support, so a user who needs it still got the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  full flash, camera shake and zoom punch. This one is fixed below.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Trapped cursor.** The system cursor is hidden for the whole page. If three.js or WebGL fails to load,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the user gets a black page, no message and no cursor. This one is fixed below.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Blank failure.** A blocked CDN or missing WebGL leaves a black page with no explanation. This one is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  fixed below.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Now the changes.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[717.327, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"/Volumes/My Shared Files\" && python3 - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[726.233, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Now the red-team tests against the real page. They measure luminance jumps around the flash in normal and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reduced-motion mode, block the CDN, and disable WebGL.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
