[1426.961, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step 6 report: Judge**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The final file is `solar-implosion.html`, now at 18 of 18 checks passing on `.ambition/tools/judge.mjs`. A\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  snapshot is in `.ambition/steps/6/`. Nothing is left running.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did.** I built `.ambition/tools/judge.mjs`, an acceptance check that renders the real page in Chrome and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  scores each promise. Where a number exists it measures the first version the same way. I ran it, fixed what\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  failed, and ran it again. I then looked at one full-loop contact sheet (`.ambition/shots/final.png`).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Promise | New | First |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Single page, three.js | pass | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | No whiteout (frames over 70% luminance, out of 100) | 1 | 32 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Vivid neon (mean saturation) | 0.28 | 0.15 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Colourful shockwave (hue bins at 9s) | 6 | 0 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Opening frame (hue bins) | 4 | 2 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Loop period exactly 10s | pass (diff 0.06) | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Seam step | 7.3 (ordinary median 6.3) | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Pointer trail, light added | +10.8 | +4.0 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Trail fades after the pointer leaves | +10.8 → +2.0 | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Core size on 640, 1920 and 390 wide screens | 0.40 / 0.40 / 0.39 | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Reduced-motion flash peak | 0.83 → 0.22 | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Particles on desktop and mobile | 91,780 vs 45,585 | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | CDN blocked: clear message, cursor kept | pass | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Hint contrast | 11.4:1 | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Console errors | none | — |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Found**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Real work fixes:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The hint text was only 3.8:1 contrast, so I raised it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The colours were washing out to pastel, so I added a saturation boost.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The opening frame was a single violet blob, so the page now opens at 8.7s. You see the rainbow shockwave\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  expanding from the first frame, and the detonation flash still plays every loop.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Test fixes:** I corrected three checks that measured the wrong thing: the camera's parallax settling,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the persistent pointer well being counted as trail, and a path crossing a planet. I changed what they\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  measure and didn't loosen any pass threshold.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **The new version is better than the first on every measured part and worse on none.** The one raw number\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  that is lower is the software-rendered frame rate: 48 fps against 53 fps. It comes from the extra effects,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and it isn't a GPU number, so your browser is the real test.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Not checked:** how the pointer feels, real GPU frame rate and sound (there is none). The pointer well\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  keeps the camera parallax, so the trail can drift slightly on screen as the camera moves. That is physically\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  coherent but may feel odd, so tell me if it does.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course.** I moved the page's opening time from 8.5s to 8.7s because of the opening-frame check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The loop itself is unchanged.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1427.062, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 7 of pushing the work further: Stretch. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1437.98, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The target.** You asked for smooth 60fps, with the particle count scaled to the device. I'm setting a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  target several times harder: **triple the particle richness, about 275,000 particles, at no more frame cost\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  than today's ~92,000.** The deadline is this step. Your fallback stays as the floor: the current build,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  which passes 18 of 18 checks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Why it should be solvable.** Particle demos routinely push millions of points because the work lives on\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the GPU. Mine is probably wasting some of it, with trig and hashing for every point every frame and heavy\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  overdraw at the sun's centre. **Where it would likely break:** if the cost is fill rate in the core, more\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  particles will cost nearly nothing, but if it's the vertex shader, tripling them will not be free without a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cheaper shader.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  First, a spike to see where the time actually goes. I'm adding a `?q=` test parameter and measuring the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  frame rate at 1×, 2× and 3× particles.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
